    <!-- http://uri.interlex.org/tgbugs/uris/readable/atlas/Space -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://uri.interlex.org/tgbugs/uris/readable/atlas/Space">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://uri.interlex.org/tgbugs/uris/readable/atlas/Artifact"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://uri.interlex.org/tgbugs/uris/readable/atlas/Space"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/BFO_0000051"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://uri.interlex.org/tgbugs/uris/readable/atlas/AnatomicalAxisAlignmentVector"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/BFO_0000051"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://uri.interlex.org/tgbugs/uris/readable/atlas/AxisDirectionMatrix"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/BFO_0000051"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://uri.interlex.org/tgbugs/uris/readable/atlas/AxisOrigin"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://uri.interlex.org/tgbugs/uris/readable/atlas/hasUnit"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://uri.interlex.org/tgbugs/uris/readable/atlas/spaceUnit"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>An anatomical space is a combination of a mathematical space with an anatomical space such that there is a defined mapping between the anatomical axes and the mathematical axes. A mathematical coordinate space by itself is NOT an anatomical space because it admits all possible orientations and reflections of the anatomical space, so for example a brain can be rotated in all directions and still be in that mathematical coordinate space. Such spaces are called unoriented spaces and are a source of significant pain when dealing with anatomical datasets.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000116>To avoid problems like those encountered by nifti an anatomical reference space MUST define an anatomical axis alignment vector which states, for example, [X, Y, Z] -&gt; [R, A, D] (left hand rule). This immediately indicates the three annotations required on any unoriented dataset as opposed to someone trying to apply the anatomical directions directly to the unoriented dataset</obo:IAO_0000116>
        <readable1:hasIlxId rdf:resource="http://uri.interlex.org/base/ilx_0777106"/>
        <readable1:localName>space</readable1:localName>
        <readable:synonym>anatomical coordinate space</readable:synonym>
        <readable:synonym>atlas space</readable:synonym>
        <readable:synonym>common coordinate space</readable:synonym>
        <readable:synonym>parameterized coordinate space</readable:synonym>
        <readable:synonym>physical space</readable:synonym>
        <rdfs:label>anatomical space</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


