    <!-- https://w3id.org/tvbo/ConnectivityMatrix -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="https://w3id.org/tvbo/ConnectivityMatrix">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/tvbo/GlobalConnectivity"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#wasDerivedFrom"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/tvbo/WhiteMatterTractography"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/tvbo/contains"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/tvbo/TractLengthsMatrix"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/tvbo/contains"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/tvbo/WeightsMatrix"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <rdfs:label>Connectivity Matrix</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:label>ConnectivityMatrix</rdfs:label>
        <skos:definition>Connectivity Matrix represents the set of all existing anatomical connections between different brain areas defined according to a given parcellation scheme and obtained from the empirical structural and diffusion MRI scans (Sporns et al., 2005; Sporns, 2011a, 2012, 2013).

It represents the topology and geometry of a BNM and consists of a set of two matrices representing the weights or strength (Weights Matrix) of the structural connections and the white matter fiber lengths (Tract Lengths Matrix). These matrices could be any of the following types of graphs: (un)weighted and (un)directed graphs (Bullmore and Bassett, 2011).

Note:
------
A TVB Image Processing Pipeline is made available via the research infrastructure EBRAINS (Schirner et al., 2020), to allow you to upload MRI data, perform preprocessing and distortion-correction on MRI data as well as white matter fiber bundle tractography on diffusion data, and get TVB-ready data set that can be directly used to simulate brain network models.</skos:definition>
        <acronym>C; SC</acronym>
        <documentation rdf:resource="https://ebrains.eu/service/the-virtual-brain#process_mri_data_sets_tvb"/>
        <identifier>000123</identifier>
        <references>Bullmore ET and Bassett DS. (2011) Brain graphs: graphical models of the human brain connectome. Annu Rev Clin Psychol; 7:113-40. https://doi.org/10.1146/annurev-clinpsy-040510-143934.

Schirner et al, 2020

Sporns O, Tononi G, Kötter R. (2005) The Human Connectome: A Structural Description of the Human Brain. Plos Comput Biol; 5 4 p. e42
https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pcbi.0010042

Sporns O. (2011a) The human connectome: a complex network. Ann NY Acad Sci; 1224: 109–125. https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1749-6632.2010.05888.x

Sporns O. (2012) Discovering the Human Connectome. MIT Press.

Sporns O. (2013) The human connectome: Origins and challenges. Neuroimage; 80:53-61 https://doi.org/10.1016/j.neuroimage.2013.03.023.</references>
        <synonym>Connectome; Structural Connectivity; Parcellated Connectome</synonym>
    </owl:Class>
    


