    <!-- https://w3id.org/tvbo/GlobalConnectivity -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="https://w3id.org/tvbo/GlobalConnectivity">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#Entity"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/tvbo/BrainNetworkModel"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/tvbo/DataTypes"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/tvbo/Network"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#used"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/tvbo/ConnectivityMatrix"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#wasDerivedFrom"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/tvbo/ConnectivityMatrix"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#wasDerivedFrom"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/tvbo/GlobalCouplingFunction"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/tvbo/has_reference"/>
                <owl:hasValue rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/tvbo/SanzLeon2013"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/tvbo/has_reference"/>
                <owl:hasValue rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/tvbo/SanzLeon2015"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/tvbo/has_reference"/>
                <owl:hasValue rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/tvbo/Wilson1972"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <rdfs:label>GlobalConnectivity</rdfs:label>
        <skos:definition>Global Connectivity describes the incoming activity coming from synaptic interactions at the macroscopic level between remote brain regions, i.e., intercortical fibers, given by the long-range white matter tracts of the order of a few centimeters (Sanz-Leon et al., 2013, 2015).

Global Connectivity introduces axonal conduction delays due to the finite propagation speed of neural activity along white matter tracts, which depend on the length of the fiber tract connecting two nodes and the transmission speed (Wilson and Cowan, 1972; Nunez, 1974).</skos:definition>
        <skos:note>Long-range connectivity are said to be heterogeneous (translationally variant; Qubbaj &amp; Jirsa, 2007; Jirsa, 2009).</skos:note>
        <skos:note>The activity propagating through long-range connections from region &apos;j&apos; to region &apos;i&apos; is the averaged activity of the set of vertices in &apos;j&apos;.</skos:note>
        <skos:note>The long-range fibers comprise the axonal processes of pyramidal cells; therefore, by convention, such a connection is an excitatory (source) to excitatory (target) interaction.
However, it is essential to note that the activity from a given region (source) may act upon inhibitory populations (target). Thus, the incoming activity transmitted through long-range fibers can generate inhibition in the target region by exciting the inhibitory population.</skos:note>
        <acronym>gc; lrc</acronym>
        <identifier>000528</identifier>
        <references>Qubbaj M and Jirsa VK. (2007) Neural field dynamics with heterogeneous connection topology. Phys Rev Lett 98.23, p. 238102. https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevLett.98.238102

Jirsa VK. (2009) Neural field dynamics with local and global connectivity and time delay. Philos Trans R Soc Lond A; 367.1891, p. 1131–1143. https://doi.org/10.1098/rsta.2008.0260

Nunez  PL. (1974) The brain wave equation: A model for the EEG. Math Biosci; 21: 279–297. https://doi.org/10.1016/0025-5564(74)90020-0</references>
        <synonym>Long-Range Connectivity</synonym>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- https://w3id.org/tvbo/GlobalCouplingFunction -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="https://w3id.org/tvbo/GlobalCouplingFunction">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/tvbo/GlobalConnectivity"/>
        <rdfs:label>GlobalCouplingFunction</rdfs:label>
        <skos:definition>Global Coupling Function is a (non-)linear function that convert the incoming activity that have been propagated over the long-range connectivity into a suitable form before entering the equations describing the local neural dynamics.</skos:definition>
        <acronym>cF</acronym>
        <expression>Expression of the Global Coupling Function.

coupling(x_i, x_j, w_ij) = coupling[ sum w_ij (x_i(t), x_j(t - tau_ij)) ]

where &apos;x_i&apos; is the current state at node &apos;i&apos;, &apos;x_j(t - tau_ij)&apos; is the (delayed) state of the coupling variable from node &apos;j&apos;, and &apos;w_ij&apos; is the connectivity weights between regions &apos;i&apos; and &apos;j&apos;.</expression>
        <identifier>000529</identifier>
        <synonym>Long-Range Coupling Function</synonym>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- https://w3id.org/tvbo/GlobalCouplingScaling -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="https://w3id.org/tvbo/GlobalCouplingScaling">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/tvbo/GlobalConnectivity"/>
        <rdfs:label>Global Coupling Scaling</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:label>GlobalCouplingScaling</rdfs:label>
        <skos:definition>Global Coupling Scaling is is a factor that rescales the global connectivity strengths.</skos:definition>
        <acronym>G</acronym>
        <acronym>K</acronym>
        <identifier>000530</identifier>
        <synonym>Global Coupling Strength</synonym>
    </owl:Class>
    


