    <!-- https://w3id.org/tvbo/NeuralFieldModel -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="https://w3id.org/tvbo/NeuralFieldModel">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/tvbo/LocalNeuralDynamics"/>
        <rdfs:label>Neural Field Model</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:label>NeuralFieldModel</rdfs:label>
        <skos:definition>Neural Field Model is an extension of neural mass model where the neuronal dynamics play out on a spatially extended cortical sheet. In other words, the model includes explicit spatial terms in addition of time, which in their differential form are typically represented by a Laplace operator (Robinson, 2012).

This type of model allows for the study of traveling waves and spatiotemporal patterns of brain activity (Coombes, 2005), either at the level of one or several circumscribed region(s) of the cortex, or for larger scale systems like the entire neocortex.</skos:definition>
        <acronym>NFM</acronym>
        <identifier>000690</identifier>
        <references>Coombes S. (2005) Waves, bumps, and patterns in neural field theories. Biol Cyber; 93, p. 91–108. https://doi.org/10.1007/s00422-005-0574-y

Robinson PA. (2012) Interrelating anatomical, effective, and functional brain connectivity using propagators and neural field theory. Phys Rev E; 85, 011912. https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevE.85.011912</references>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- https://w3id.org/tvbo/NeuralMassModel -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="https://w3id.org/tvbo/NeuralMassModel">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/tvbo/LocalNeuralDynamics"/>
        <rdfs:label>Neural Mass Model</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:label>NeuralMassModel</rdfs:label>
        <skos:definition>Neural Mass Model (NMM) is a lumped representation of the dynamics of a group of neurons with similar characteristics and coordinated behaviors via a single common variable (Wilson and Cowan, 1972, 1973; Jansen et al., 1993; Jansen and Rit, 1995; Spiegler et al., 2011; Liley et al., 1999; Stefanescu and Jirsa, 2008, 2011).

The model typically describes either the average firing rate and mean postsynaptic potential under the assumption of random firings of neuronal spikes (activity-based models; Freeman, 1975, 1992; da Silva et al., 1974), or population model accounting for parameter dispersion in the neuron membrane potential parameters and giving rise to multiple mass actions (voltage-based models; Assisi et al., 2005; Stefanescu and Jirsa, 2008, 2010, 2011). More generally these models can be classified as activity-based or voltage-based models (Ermentrout and Kopell, 1998). Such model is derived following the so-called mean-field (Wilson and Cowan, 1973) or mass-action (Freeman, 1975) formalisms.

Some NMMs are purely phenomenological or abstract, that is, although they qualitatively reproduce the dynamics of a certain physiological variable, the connection between physiological measurements and the model parameters cannot be made directly.</skos:definition>
        <skos:note>NMM presented, here, encompasses an approximation to neural-field modeling by using networks of NMs on a mesh (Bojak et al., 2011; Spiegler and Jirsa, 2013).</skos:note>
        <acronym>NMM</acronym>
        <identifier>000691</identifier>
        <references>Assisi C, Jirsa VK, Kelso J (2005). Synchrony and clustering in heterogeneous networks with global coupling and parameter dispersion. Phys. Rev. Lett. 94 (1), 94. https://doi.org/10.1103/physrevlett.94.018106

da Silva FHL, Hoeks A, Smits H, Zetterberg LH. (1974) Model of brain rhythmic activity. Kybern; 15 (1), p. 27–37. https://doi.org/10.1007/BF00270757.

Ermentrout GB and Kopell N (1998). Fine structure of neural spiking and synchronization in the presence of conduction delays. Proc. Natl. Acad. Sci. U. S. A. 95 (3), 1259–1264. https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.95.3.1259

Freeman WJ (1975) Mass Action in the Nervous System. New York: Academic Press.

Freeman WJ (1992) Tutorial on Neurobiology: from Single Neurons to Brain Chaos. Int J Bifurc Chaos; 2: 451-482. https://doi.org/10.1142/S0218127492000653

Jansen B and Rit V (1995). Electroencephalogramand visual evoked potential generation in a mathematical model of coupled cortical columns. Biol. Cybern. 73 (4), 357–366. https://doi.org/10.1007/bf00199471

Jansen B, Zouridakis G, Brandt M (1993). A neurophysiologically-based mathematical model of flash visual evoked potentials. Biol. Cybern. 68 (3), 275–283. https://doi.org/10.1007/BF00224863

Liley DT, Alexander DM, Wright JJ, Aldous MD (1999). Alpha rhythm emerges from large-scale networks of realistically coupled multicompartmental model cortical neurons. Network 10 (1), 79–92. https://doi.org/10.1088/0954-898X/10/1/005

Spiegler A and Jirsa VK (2013) Systematic approximations of neural fields through networks of neural masses in the virtual brain. Neuroimage; 83C, p. 704–725. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.neuroimage.2013.06.018

Spiegler A, Knösche TR, Schwab K, Haueisen J, Atay FM (2011). Modeling brain resonance phenomena using a neural mass model. PLoS Comput. Biol. 7 (12), e1002298. https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pcbi.1002298

Stefanescu R and Jirsa VK (2011). Reduced representations of heterogeneous mixed neural networks with synaptic coupling. Phys. Rev. E Stat. Nonlinear Soft Matter Phys. 83 (2). https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevE.83.026204

Stefanescu R and Jirsa VK (2010). Neural Population Modes Capture Biologically Realistic Large Scale Network Dynamics. Bull Math Biol 73, 325–343. https://doi.org/10.1007/s11538-010-9573-9

Stefanescu R and Jirsa V (2008). A low dimensional description of globally coupled heterogeneous
neural networks of excitatory and inhibitory. PLoS Comput. Biol. 4 (11), 26–36. https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pcbi.1000219

Wilson H and Cowan J (1973) A mathematical theory of the functional dynamics of cortical and thalamic nervous tissue. Kybernetik; 13, p. 55–80. https://dx.doi.org/10.1007/BF00288786

Wilson H and Cowan J (1972). Excitatory and inhibitory interactions in localized populations of model neurons. Biophys. J. 12 (1), 1–24. https://doi.org/10.1016/S0006-3495(72)86068-5</references>
        <synonym>Lumped Model</synonym>
        <synonym>Neural Population Model</synonym>
    </owl:Class>
    


