    <!-- https://w3id.org/tvbo/NullclineProperties -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="https://w3id.org/tvbo/NullclineProperties">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/tvbo/DynamicalParameter"/>
        <dc:description>Curves for the rate of change of a variable.</dc:description>
        <rdfs:label>Nullcline Properties</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:label>NullclineProperties</rdfs:label>
        <identifier>000698</identifier>
        <wiki>## Nullclines

Nullclines are an important concept in the study of dynamical systems, particularly in the analysis of systems of ordinary differential equations (ODEs). They provide insights into the behavior of trajectories and can be used to predict steady states, periodic orbits, and more.

### Definition
Nullclines are curves in the phase plane (or space) on which one of the system&apos;s differential equations equals zero, i.e., one of the derivatives is zero. The nullclines can be thought of as dividing lines that show when a variable&apos;s rate of change is zero.

In a system of two first-order ODEs:

\[
\begin{align*}
\frac{dx}{dt} &amp;= f(x,y) \\
\frac{dy}{dt} &amp;= g(x,y)
\end{align*}
\]

**X-nullcline** is the set of points where:

\[
f(x,y) = 0
\]

Similarly, the **Y-nullcline** is the set of points where:

\[
g(x,y) = 0
\]

### Properties

1. **Intersections of nullclines:** The points where the x- and y-nullclines intersect are called fixed points or equilibria. This is because at these points, both \(\frac{dx}{dt} = 0\) and \(\frac{dy}{dt} = 0\), indicating no change in either variable.

2. **Trajectory behavior:**
    - Along the x-nullcline, trajectories are vertical since \(\frac{dx}{dt} = 0\).
    - Along the y-nullcline, trajectories are horizontal since \(\frac{dy}{dt} = 0\).

3. **Determination of system behavior:** By analyzing the signs of \(f\) and \(g\) around the nullclines, we can determine the direction of trajectories in the phase plane. For example, if \(f &gt; 0\) and \(g &gt; 0\), trajectories will be moving up and to the right.

4. **Stability analysis:** The stability of fixed points (intersections of nullclines) can be determined by analyzing the eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix at these points.

### Applications

Nullclines are used extensively in:
- Biology, to understand dynamical behaviors in ecological or neural models.
- Engineering, in the analysis of control systems.
- Physics, to study various dynamical systems.

### Visual Representation

Plotting nullclines in the phase plane gives a visual representation of the system’s dynamics. By analyzing the regions between the nullclines, one can deduce the behavior of trajectories.</wiki>
    </owl:Class>
    


